4.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: {Colleagues 1, 3 and 5 continue to defend C on the narrow reading that any non-voting authorization satisfies requirement 4, but that reading ignores the partners’ explicit desire for a workable “option of bringing in other investors.” A class deliberately stripped of both dividends and any residual claim on dissolution assets is not a realistic vehicle for future capital; outside investors will simply decline to participate, undermining the very flexibility the partners want.  

A avoids that practical trap while still meeting every listed condition. The two identical voting classes (A and B) are issued 50/50 to Tyler personally and to Alex’s holding company, preserving equal board control through the two sole directors. Tyler can receive dividends directly on his Class B shares; Alex can be compensated exclusively through salary or director fees paid by the operating company to the holding company, keeping his compensation path clean. Class C is already authorized as non-voting, giving the partners a blank slate they can later amend or tailor with whatever economic rights are needed to attract passive investors—without baking in zero-value terms from day one.  

The holding-company route for Alex also supplies legitimate tax-planning flexibility that C’s structure does not address. B still contains the internal drafting inconsistency on Class B rights and reverses the dividend assignment; D and E lack a non-voting class or equal control, respectively. On balance, A is the only structure that simultaneously satisfies the four requirements without creating an investor class that is commercially unusable from the outset.}

