5.text:
C

The persistent split reduces to whether requirement 4 is read literally (a non-voting class must simply be pre-authorized) or with an added commercial-attractiveness gloss (the class must already carry economic rights investors would actually accept). The question text supplies only the former; nothing in the four enumerated conditions demands that the non-voting class already embed dividends or residual claims.  

C is therefore the sole structure that meets every stated element simultaneously and without contradiction:  
- equal board control is preserved by the two sole directors plus 50/50 voting shares;  
- Alex’s compensation path is enabled by non-dividend Class A;  
- Tyler’s path is enabled by dividend-eligible Class B;  
- Class C is already authorized as non-voting, satisfying the “no say in operations” clause for future investors.  

A still assigns identical dividend rights to both voting classes (eliminating the required payout distinction) and inserts an unrequested spousal holding company. B reverses the dividend assignment and contains an internal inconsistency on Class B rights. D omits any non-voting class. E adds extra directors, breaking equal control.  

Because the prompt supplies no basis for importing an “investor economics” filter that the drafters could have stated but did not, C remains the unique match.

